Supreme People's Court

Notice on Issuing the "Guiding Opinions on Strengthening and Standardizing the Work of Case Elevation of Jurisdiction and Retrial Adjudication by Higher Courts"

Fa Fa [2023] No. 13

To: Higher People's Courts of all provinces, autonomous regions, and municipalities directly under the Central Government; the Military Court of the People's Liberation Army; the Production and Construction Corps Branch of the Higher People's Court of the Xinjiang Uygur Autonomous Region:

The "Guiding Opinions on Strengthening and Standardizing the Work of Case Elevation of Jurisdiction and Retrial Adjudication by Higher Courts" are hereby issued for your earnest implementation in light of actual practice. Any problems encountered during implementation shall be promptly reported to the Supreme People's Court.

July 28, 2023

Guiding Opinions on Strengthening and Standardizing the Work of Case Elevation of Jurisdiction and Retrial Adjudication by Higher Courts

To strengthen the construction of the hierarchical judicial supervision system of the People's Courts, to deeply and substantively realize the new era of active justice, and to promote the modernization of adjudication work to support and guarantee Chinese-style modernization, these Opinions are formulated in accordance with the provisions of relevant laws and judicial interpretations, and in light of the actual work of adjudication, to strengthen and standardize the work of case elevation of jurisdiction and retrial adjudication by higher courts in the People's Courts.

I. General Provisions

Article 1: Improving and perfecting the working mechanism for case elevation of jurisdiction and retrial adjudication by higher courts is an important part of the reform to improve the functional positioning of the four-level court system. It is conducive to promoting the governance of litigation sources, unifying the application of law, and protecting the rights and interests of the people. People's courts at all levels shall, through active, standardized, and reasonable application of elevation of jurisdiction, promote the transfer of cases that have guiding significance, involve major interests, or may be subject to interference to higher-level courts for trial, giving full play to the exemplary and leading role of typical case judgments, and achieving the organic unity of political, social, and legal effects. Intermediate people's courts and above shall increase the application of retrial adjudication by higher courts, accurately fulfilling the functions of hierarchical supervision and retrial error correction. The Supreme People's Court focuses on adjudicating typical cases with universal guiding significance for the application of law and major divergences in the application of law, giving full play to the functions of the highest judicial organ in supervising and guiding the national adjudication work and ensuring the correct and uniform application of law.

Article 2: The term "elevation of jurisdiction" as used in these Opinions refers to, in accordance with the provisions of Article 24 of the Criminal Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, Article 39 of the Civil Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, and Article 24 of the Administrative Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, the transfer of a first-instance case under the jurisdiction of a lower people's court to a higher people's court for trial, including elevation of jurisdiction upon the request of the lower people's court and elevation of jurisdiction ex officio by the higher people's court.

Article 3: The term "retrial adjudication by higher courts" as used in these Opinions refers to, in accordance with the provisions of Article 205, Paragraph 2, and Article 211, Paragraph 2 of the Civil Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, and Article 91 and Article 92, Paragraph 2 of the Administrative Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, where a higher people's court, finding that a civil or administrative judgment or ruling of a lower people's court that has become legally effective is indeed erroneous and deems it necessary to adjudicate the case by a higher court, issues a ruling to retry the case by its own court, including retrial adjudication ex officio by the higher people's court, retrial adjudication upon the application of the parties for retrial, and retrial adjudication by the Supreme People's Court upon the request of a higher people's court.

II. Improving the Mechanism for Elevation of Jurisdiction

Article 4: A lower people's court that has accepted a first-instance criminal, civil, or administrative case and considers that it falls under any of the following circumstances and is not suitable for trial by its own court shall request the higher people's court to try the case:

(1) Involving major national interests or public interests;
(2) Being a new type of case within the jurisdiction and the facts of the case are complex and difficult;
(3) Having the effect of governance of litigation sources, being conducive to forming exemplary judgments, and promoting the uniform, efficient, and proper resolution of similar disputes;
(4) Having guiding significance for the application of law;
(5) Where there are major divergences in the application of law among similar cases that have become effective in judgments or rulings within the higher people's court or its jurisdictional lower courts within the past three years;
(6) Where trial by the higher people's court at first instance is more conducive to a fair trial.

The higher people's court may decide to elevate jurisdiction over a first-instance criminal, civil, or administrative case already accepted by a lower people's court within its jurisdiction if it considers that the case falls under any of the above circumstances and it is necessary for its own court to try it.

Article 5: "Cases that are new types within the jurisdiction and have complex and difficult facts" mainly refer to cases where the field involved, legal relationships, or scope of regulation have a first-case effect or are relatively rare within the jurisdiction, and there are difficulties and controversies in the application of law.

"Cases that have the effect of governance of litigation sources, are conducive to forming exemplary judgments, and promote the uniform, efficient, and proper resolution of similar disputes" refer to cases with exemplary guiding value, which, by establishing the adjudication rules of typical cases, can provide normative guidance for handling similar disputes, guide parties to make rational choices, promote the systematic resolution of batch disputes, and achieve source governance of disputes.

"Cases that have guiding significance for the application of law" refer to cases where laws, regulations, judicial interpretations, or judicial guidance documents do not contain clear provisions, and the application of law needs to be further clarified through the adjudication of typical cases; or where the objective circumstances on the basis of which judicial interpretations, judicial guidance documents, or guiding cases were issued have undergone significant changes, and continuing to apply the relevant rules for adjudication is clearly contrary to fairness and justice.

"Cases where trial by the higher people's court at first instance is more conducive to a fair trial" refer to cases where, due to factors such as the field involved, the subject matter, or the interests, may be subject to local influence or external interference, and the lower people's court is not suitable to exercise jurisdiction.

Article 6: A lower people's court requesting elevation of jurisdiction by a higher people's court shall, upon approval by the president or the vice president in charge of the court, submit a written request. The request shall include the basic facts of the case, the facts and reasons for requesting elevation of jurisdiction, and necessary case materials.

Article 7: For a civil or administrative first-instance case requesting elevation of jurisdiction, the request shall be made to the higher people's court after the expiration of the defense period and no later than thirty days before the expiration of the statutory trial period of the case.

For a criminal first-instance case requesting elevation of jurisdiction, the request shall be made to the higher people's court no later than fifteen days before the expiration of the statutory trial period of the case.

Article 8: Upon receiving the request and materials for case elevation of jurisdiction, the higher people's court shall, by the case filing division, assign a case number with the prefix "Xia" and transfer it to the relevant adjudication division to form a collegial panel for review. The higher people's court shall complete the review within thirty days from the date of case number assignment, unless otherwise provided by law or judicial interpretation regarding the review time limit.

After review and upon approval by the president or the vice president in charge of the court, the collegial panel shall, in accordance with the litigation document formats attached to these Opinions, issue a legal document either approving or not approving the elevation of jurisdiction. Such legal documents shall take effect immediately upon issuance.

Article 9: A higher people's court that discovers, through the channels referred to in Article 21 of these Opinions, that a first-instance case accepted by a lower people's court may require elevation of jurisdiction may promptly communicate with the relevant lower people's court and issue a written notice requesting the provision of necessary case materials.

If the higher people's court considers that the case should be subject to elevation of jurisdiction, upon approval by the president or the vice president in charge of the court, it shall, in accordance with the litigation document formats attached to these Opinions, issue a legal document for elevation of jurisdiction.

Article 10: The legal document for elevation of jurisdiction issued by the higher people's court shall contain the following:

(1) Basic information of the case;
(2) The reasons and analysis of the opinion of the court for deciding on elevation of jurisdiction.

If the higher people's court does not approve the elevation of jurisdiction, it shall state the reasons and analysis of the opinion in the relevant legal document.

Article 11: After deciding on elevation of jurisdiction, the higher people's court shall, within five days of issuing the legal document, deliver the legal document to the originally accepting lower people's court. Upon receiving the legal document for elevation of jurisdiction, the originally accepting lower people's court shall deliver it to the parties within five days and transfer the case file materials to the higher people's court within ten days. The higher people's court shall docket the case within five days of receiving the case file materials. For cases initiated by a procuratorial organ by way of public prosecution, if the higher people's court decides to elevate jurisdiction, it shall notify the procuratorial organ at the same level in writing, and the originally accepting lower people's court shall return the case file materials to the procuratorial organ at the same level and notify the parties in writing.

If the higher people's court decides not to elevate jurisdiction, it shall, within five days of issuing the legal document, deliver the legal document to the originally accepting lower people's court and return the relevant case file materials. The case shall continue to be tried by the originally accepting lower people's court.

Article 12: For cases where the higher people's court decides on elevation of jurisdiction, it shall legally form a collegial panel to try the case under the general procedure of first instance.

Procedural work already legally completed by the originally accepting lower people's court, such as service, preservation, and appraisal, may not be repeated by the higher people's court.

Article 13: For cases where an intermediate people's court or a higher people's court decides on elevation of jurisdiction, it shall file a record with the case filing division of the higher people's court.

Article 14: For cases subject to elevation of jurisdiction in accordance with these Opinions, the trial period shall be recalculated from the date of docketing by the higher people's court.

The period during which the lower people's court submits a request for elevation of jurisdiction to the higher people's court and the period during which the higher people's court reviews and handles the request shall not be included in the trial period of the case.

For cases where the request for elevation of jurisdiction is not approved, the computation of the trial period for the case shall resume from the date the originally accepting lower people's court receives the relevant legal document.

III. Standardizing the Mechanism for Retrial Adjudication by Higher Courts in Civil and Administrative Cases

Article 15: A higher people's court that finds that a civil or administrative judgment or ruling of a lower people's court that has become legally effective meets the conditions for retrial shall generally adjudicate the case by a higher court.

For civil or administrative judgments or rulings that meet the conditions for retrial, if any of the following circumstances exist, the Supreme People's Court or the higher people's court may remand the case to the original trial people's court for retrial, or designate another people's court at the same level as the original trial people's court for retrial, unless otherwise provided by law or judicial interpretation:

(1) The main evidence for finding the facts in the original judgment or ruling was not subject to cross-examination;
(2) For the main evidence needed to try the case, the parties could not collect it on their own due to objective reasons and applied in writing to the court for investigation and collection, but the court did not investigate or collect it;
(3) Violating legal provisions and depriving the parties of their right to debate;
(4) The judgment or ruling that has become legally effective was made by a first-instance court;
(5) Civil cases where one party has a large number of persons or both parties are citizens;
(6) Other circumstances decided by the adjudication committee after discussion.

Article 16: For civil and administrative retrial application review cases legally accepted by the Supreme People's Court, in addition to circumstances where retrial adjudication by a higher court is required by law or judicial interpretation, if any of the following circumstances exist, the Supreme People's Court shall also issue a ruling for retrial adjudication by a higher court:

(1) Having a significant national impact;
(2) Having universal guiding significance for the application of law;
(3) The issue of application of law involved has significant divergence within the Supreme People's Court itself;
(4) The issue of application of law involved has significant divergence among similar cases that have become effective in judgments or rulings between different higher people's courts;
(5) Retrial adjudication by the Supreme People's Court is more conducive to a fair trial of the case;
(6) Other circumstances where the Supreme People's Court considers that retrial adjudication by a higher court is necessary.

If the Supreme People's Court ex officio discovers that a civil or administrative judgment or ruling of a local people's court at any level that has become legally effective is indeed erroneous and meets the provisions of the preceding paragraph, it shall adjudicate the case by a higher court.

Article 17: If a higher people's court finds that a civil or administrative judgment or ruling made by its own court or a lower people's court within its jurisdiction that has become legally effective is indeed erroneous in the application of law and falls under any of the circumstances listed in Article 16, Paragraph 1, Items 1 to 5 of these Opinions, it may, after discussion and decision by its own adjudication committee, request the Supreme People's Court for retrial adjudication.

Article 18: When a higher people's court requests the Supreme People's Court for retrial adjudication, it shall submit a written request to the Supreme People's Court, which shall include the following:

(1) Basic facts of the case;
(2) The status of the retrial application review by the court;
(3) Reasons for requesting retrial adjudication;
(4) Opinions of the collegial panel and the discussion opinions of the adjudication committee;
(5) Necessary case materials.

Article 19: Upon receiving the request for retrial adjudication and materials submitted by the higher people's court, the Supreme People's Court shall, by the case filing division, assign a case number with the prefix "Jian" and transfer it to the relevant adjudication division to form a collegial panel for review. The review shall be completed within three months, and the following actions shall be taken:

(1) If the conditions for retrial adjudication are met, a ruling for retrial adjudication shall be issued;
(2) If the conditions for retrial adjudication are not met, a reply disapproving the retrial adjudication shall be issued.

If the Supreme People's Court disapproves the retrial adjudication, it shall state its opinions and reasons in the reply.

Article 20: The period during which a case is submitted to the Supreme People's Court for request of retrial adjudication and the period during which the Supreme People's Court reviews and handles it shall not be included in the processing period of the case for retrial application review.

For cases where the retrial adjudication is disapproved, the computation of the processing period for the retrial application review case shall resume from the date the higher people's court receives the reply.

IV. Improving the Safeguard Mechanisms for Elevation of Jurisdiction and Retrial Adjudication by Higher Courts

Article 21: Higher people's courts shall improve and perfect the mechanisms for discovering, monitoring, and identifying special types of cases, and shall actively initiate elevation of jurisdiction or retrial adjudication proceedings through the following channels:

(1) Handling requests from lower people's courts regarding issues of application of law;
(2) Conducting judicial supervision inspections, judicial patrols, and case evaluations;
(3) Handling opinions of procuratorial supervision;
(4) Handling matters or issues of concern to representatives of the People's Congress and members of the Chinese People's Political Consultative Conference;
(5) Handling letters and visits from the public concerning specific cases;
(6) Processing requests from parties for elevation of jurisdiction or retrial adjudication;
(7) Monitoring public opinion on cases;
(8) Handling other matters transferred by relevant state organs or social organizations.

Article 22: For cases subject to elevation of jurisdiction or retrial adjudication, the relevant people's courts shall strengthen supervision and management, and improve the incentive and assessment mechanisms accordingly. The rule-setting significance, guidance effect on lower courts, effectiveness in governance of litigation sources, conversion of outcomes, and public reaction of cases subject to elevation of jurisdiction or retrial adjudication shall be taken as important evaluation criteria.

Article 23: Each adjudication division of the Supreme People's Court shall strengthen supervision and guidance over lower courts, coordinate the work of elevation of jurisdiction and retrial adjudication for cases in its own adjudication line, fully grasp the situation of cases, and promptly handle request matters. Each higher people's court shall regularly report to the Supreme People's Court on the circumstances of cases subject to elevation of jurisdiction, strengthen communication, problem feedback, and professional guidance among the various adjudication business lines of lower people's courts within its jurisdiction, and refine and clarify the scope, circumstances, and procedures of cases subject to elevation of jurisdiction and retrial adjudication in light of the actual work of adjudication within its jurisdiction.

Article 24: The Supreme People's Court and higher people's courts shall improve and perfect the mechanism for converting the adjudication rules of cases subject to elevation of jurisdiction and retrial adjudication, uniformly include the adjudications of cases subject to elevation of jurisdiction into the Case Database of the People's Courts, actively cultivate cases subject to elevation of jurisdiction and retrial adjudication that have guiding significance for the application of law as guiding cases or reference cases, and promote the conversion of adjudications with rule-establishing significance and exemplary guiding roles into judicial interpretations, judicial guidance documents, judicial suggestions, mediation guidelines, etc. Increase publicity efforts for cases subject to elevation of jurisdiction and retrial adjudication, focusing publicity on enhancing the sense of gain of the people, promoting the improvement of judicial credibility, and effectively breaking the phenomenon of "litigation home and away," actively through trial openness, reasoning in judgments, case release, news reports, seminars and exchanges, etc., fully demonstrate the effectiveness of relevant adjudication work, promote the cultivation of public and social legal awareness, and create a favorable atmosphere for the orderly promotion of relevant work.

V. Supplementary Provisions

Article 25: These Opinions shall be interpreted by the Supreme People's Court. Each higher people's court may, in accordance with relevant laws, judicial interpretations, and these Opinions, and in light of the actual work of adjudication, formulate or revise implementing rules for elevation of jurisdiction and retrial adjudication within its region, and report them to the Supreme People's Court for record.

Article 26: These Opinions shall come into effect on August 1, 2023. Any previous provisions inconsistent with these Opinions shall be governed by these Opinions.

